# Vertical content rules

Keep the `Vertical` type, slugs, and category taxonomy (Insurance | Legal | Home Services | Finance | Other) exactly as they are. Rewrite values only.

## Buyer-first weighting

`description` and `summary` speak to the BUYER first: what demand exists, who's calling, why intent is high, what filters/models are available (CPL, cost per call, traffic). `publisherFit` stays one tight sentence — that's the 20%.

## Anti-clone rule (critical)

Every vertical must contain at least two facts that are true ONLY for that vertical — otherwise it's a find-and-replace page and Google treats it as thin. Examples of vertical-specific angles:

- Auto insurance: continuous shopping behavior, rate-increase-driven switching, state minimum coverage differences
- Health insurance: open enrollment vs SEP timing, ACA vs short-term product split, licensing constraints on buyers
- Medicare Advantage: AEP/OEP seasonality, CMS marketing rules shaping creative and routing
- Final expense: older demographic, phone-first buyers, smaller policies but fast decisions
- Personal injury: case-type qualification (statute of limitations, injury type, retained-attorney screening), high value per signed case
- Workers' compensation / SSDI / bankruptcy: claim status, prior representation, and product-specific screens
- Debt settlement / tax relief: minimum debt thresholds as the core qualifier
- Solar / roofing / HVAC / plumbing / windows / water damage: geo-dependence, homeownership qualification, storm/season demand spikes
- Mortgage / personal loans / credit repair: credit band and product-purpose filters
- Education / trade schools / online education: program and start-date matching for enrollment teams

Never invent RidgeRise-specific volumes, payouts, or conversion rates for any vertical.

## Field style

- `summary`: ≤ 12 words, the caller's intent in plain language.
- `description`: 1–3 sentences — who is calling, why now, what a buyer can filter on.
- `buyerFit`: name the actual buyer types (carriers, agencies, IMOs, intake teams, law firms, contractors, call centers).
- `publisherFit`: the traffic types that get approved (search, native, social, SEO/content, call paths).
- `keywords`: 2–4 phrases with buying intent for that vertical ("buy auto insurance calls", "final expense live transfers") — not generic informational terms.

## Detail page SEO

Title pattern: "{Name} Pay Per Call & Leads" or "{Name} Calls for {buyer type}" (Seo component appends the brand). Description mentions both CPL and cost-per-call availability when true for the vertical.
